-- title:
--   Quotient Hopf algebra by a Hopf ideal over a commutative ring
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $A$ a commutative $R$-algebra carrying a Hopf algebra structure over $R$, and let $I \subseteq A$ be an ideal satisfying the three conditions: the image of $\Delta a$ under the map $A \otimes_R A \to (A/I) \otimes_R (A/I)$ induced by the quotient $R$-algebra map $\pi_I : A \to A/I$ on both factors vanishes for every $a \in I$; $\varepsilon(a) = 0$ for every $a \in I$; and the antipode maps $I$ into $I$. Then there exist a type $A'$ with a commutative ring structure and a Hopf $R$-algebra structure, and an $R$-bialgebra homomorphism $\pi : A \to A'$, such that $\pi$ is surjective, the kernel of $\pi$ as a ring homomorphism equals $I$, $A'$ is cocommutative whenever $A$ is, $A'$ is a finite $R$-module whenever $A$ is, and $\pi$ has the universal property of the quotient in commutative $R$-algebras: for every commutative $R$-algebra $T$ and every $R$-algebra homomorphism $\varphi : A \to T$ with $I$ contained in the kernel of $\varphi$, there is a unique $R$-algebra homomorphism $\varphi' : A' \to T$ with $\varphi'$ composed after $\pi$ equal to $\varphi$. Note that $A'$ is produced existentially rather than as a named construction, and lies in the same universe as $A$.
--
--   This is the standard statement that an ideal of a commutative Hopf algebra which is a Hopf ideal (comultiplication lands in $I \otimes A + A \otimes I$, counit kills $I$, antipode preserves $I$) admits a Hopf algebra quotient — dually, that $\operatorname{Spec}(A/I)$ is a closed subgroup scheme of $\operatorname{Spec} A$ — here over an arbitrary commutative base ring rather than a field. It is used in the construction of quotients of finite flat group schemes in this development, for instance in passing to the quotient by the ideal generated by the augmentation ideal's image and in producing connected–étale sequences over $\mathbb{Z}_p$.
